      "basis": "Select John Glasgow-2475 of the Irvine Isabell Kyle/Kelle household as the possible working father of James Glasgow-4157, Helen Nasmith’s partner, at 58% subjective confidence. The leading explanation identifies the later James with John and Isabell’s James, baptised at Irvine on 19 August 1711 (Glasgow-4100). This joins a specific recorded birth family to a specific later household in the same parish. Assuming infant baptism, that James would be about thirty when John was born in August 1741 and about thirty-five at Susanna’s 1747 birth. The later household’s first known son is John, agreeing with the proposed paternal grandfather. Same-parish continuity, a comfortable adult chronology and this modest naming clue together favour this family over the younger 1721 comparison. None independently proves the identification.",
      "limitations": "The 58% assesses the complete father proposition, including the uncertain baptism-to-adult identity. No source names the 1741–1747 father’s own parents, and no residence, trade or witness links the households. Baptism is not necessarily near birth, John is a common forename, and the first known child is not necessarily the eldest child. The 1711 and 1721 ages are illustrative infant-baptism assumptions, not recorded ages. James Glasgow-4157’s before-1722 birth is an inherited adult-age estimate, not a 1722 birth or a hard biological boundary. Do not use that estimate to prefer or exclude a candidate. All dates and identities remain unchanged. The original registers are uninspected; maintained Findmypast transcripts are being reassessed, not newly discovered.",
